🔹 3. List Interface Methods
____________________________________________________
Here’s a complete list of important methods, grouped by type:

A. Adding Elements
Method	Description
add(E e)	Adds element at the end
add(int index, E element)	Adds element at specific index
addAll(Collection<? extends E> c)	Adds all elements from another collection
addAll(int index, Collection<? extends E> c)	Adds all elements at a specific index

Example:

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("Apple");
list.add("Banana");
list.add(1, "Mango"); // adds at index 1
System.out.println(list); // [Apple, Mango, Banana]

B. Accessing Elements
Method	Description
get(int index)	Returns element at index
indexOf(Object o)	Returns first occurrence index
lastIndexOf(Object o)	Returns last occurrence index
size()	Returns number of elements

Example:

System.out.println(list.get(1));       // Mango
System.out.println(list.indexOf("Banana")); // 2
System.out.println(list.size());        // 3

C. Removing Elements
Method	Description
remove(int index)	Removes element at given index
remove(Object o)	Removes first occurrence of object
removeAll(Collection<?> c)	Removes all elements present in another collection
clear()	Removes all elements

Example:

list.remove("Mango");   // removes Mango
list.remove(0);         // removes element at index 0
System.out.println(list); // [Banana]

D. Replacing/Updating Elements
Method	Description
set(int index, E element)	Replaces element at index with new element

Example:

list.set(0, "Grapes");
System.out.println(list); // [Grapes]

E. Sublist / Range Operations
Method	Description
subList(int fromIndex, int toIndex)	Returns a view of a portion of the list

Example:

list.add("Orange");
list.add("Apple");
List<String> sub = list.subList(0, 2);
System.out.println(sub); // [Grapes, Orange]

F. Iteration Methods
Using for-each loop
for(String fruit : list) {
    System.out.println(fruit);
}

Using Iterator
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while(it.hasNext()) {
    System.out.println(it.next());
}

Using ListIterator (forward and backward)
ListIterator<String> listIt = list.listIterator();
while(listIt.hasNext()) {
    System.out.println(listIt.next());
}
while(listIt.hasPrevious()) {
    System.out.println(listIt.previous());
}

G. Other Useful Methods
Method	Description
contains(Object o)	Checks if element exists
isEmpty()	Checks if list is empty
toArray()	Converts list to array
retainAll(Collection<?> c)	Keeps only elements present in another collection
equals(Object o)	Checks equality with another list

Example:

System.out.println(list.contains("Apple")); // true
System.out.println(list.isEmpty());         // false

🔹 4. Implementing Classes Comparison
Class	Features	Use Case
ArrayList	Resizable array, fast random access	Best for read-heavy operations
LinkedList	Doubly linked list, fast insert/delete	Best for frequent add/remove
Vector	Synchronized ArrayList (legacy)	Thread-safe, rarely used now
Stack	LIFO, extends Vector	Classic stack operations
________________________________________________

List Interface: Ordered collection, allows duplicates, index-based access.
Common Implementations: ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector, Stack.
Key Methods:
add(), get(), set(), remove(), subList(), contains(), isEmpty(), size().
Iteration: for-each, Iterator, ListIterator.
Use Case: Use when sequence/order matters and duplicates are allowed.

